From ba2d57a0768c547844f0cdca3f834143b450c8d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:44:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] RISC OS: present only what changed

SDL's software renderer converted and scaled the whole frame every frame.
With a display scale that isn't a whole number (150%), linear filtering
and the RISC OS screen's XBGR order it takes SDL's slowest path: on a Pi 4
at 1920 x 1200 the game ran at 13 frames a second, 78 ms a frame, nearly
all of it presenting (on Linux the city takes 0.7 ms to draw and SDL 18 ms
to present).

On RISC OS (or with -DRISCOS_PRESENT_TEST=ON for testing elsewhere) there
is no SDL renderer: src/platform/riscos/riscos_present.c keeps a copy of
the last frame, finds the 64 x 16 pixel cells that changed, and only
scales (nearest neighbour), converts (red and blue swapped for XBGR) and
updates those, as runs joined into rectangles. Other surface formats go
through SDL_BlitScaled. Mouse positions are mapped from the window to the
canvas, which SDL's renderer used to do. The game's pointer is blended
into the canvas just for presenting and taken out again.

A "Present:" log line every 10 seconds gives frames, full frames, cells
changed, pixels drawn and time per frame.

Host checks: at 100% the picture is identical to SDL's renderer's (0
pixels differ); forced XBGR order gives exactly the reference with red
and blue swapped; a full 1920 x 1200 frame at 150% takes 1.2 ms to
convert and scale (SDL: 18 ms); a still city 0.3-0.7 ms.
